<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-documents.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-documents-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-documents.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/appraisal-specialties/">Specialties</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Umpire Services</span></nav><p class="tag">Appraisal specialty</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Umpire Services in Hawaii</h1><p class="lede">When two appraisers can’t agree, the umpire breaks the deadlock. We serve as a neutral umpire on property appraisals across the islands.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Neutral third member</dd></div><div><dt>Selected by</dt><dd>Both appraisers</dd></div><div><dt>Decides</dt><dd>Disputed items</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What an umpire does</h2>
<p>Under a typical appraisal clause, each side appoints its own appraiser, and those two appraisers select an umpire. If the appraisers reach agreement on their own, the umpire may never be needed. If they cannot, they submit their differences to the umpire, and an award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>The umpire’s job is to be neutral. An umpire does not represent the policyholder or the insurer, does not advocate for either estimate and does not decide coverage. The role is limited to weighing the evidence on disputed items and helping the panel reach a signed award.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">One role per claim</b>On any given claim, we serve in only one role. If we are a party’s appraiser, we will not act as umpire on that claim, and if we are the umpire, we have no advocacy role. We disclose any prior relationship with the parties or appraisers before accepting.</div>
<h2>How we work as umpire</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Disclosure first:</strong> we check for conflicts and disclose any relationships before accepting the appointment.</li>
<li><strong>Clear ground rules:</strong> we confirm the scope of the dispute, the items in contention and how submissions will be exchanged.</li>
<li><strong>Review both positions:</strong> each appraiser’s estimate, photos, measurements and supporting documentation.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect when helpful:</strong> a site visit often resolves disagreements about measurements, materials or the extent of damage.</li>
<li><strong>Item-by-item decisions:</strong> we work through disputed line items and explain the reasoning to both appraisers.</li>
<li><strong>Written award:</strong> we prepare an award in the format the policy requires, for signature by at least two panel members.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Hawaii knowledge matters in an umpire</h2>
<p>An umpire unfamiliar with the islands may not appreciate why a corrugated metal roof on the windward side wears differently than one in Kapolei, why freight to Lanai or Molokai changes a price, or how General Excise Tax applies to contractor work. Knowing single-wall construction, post-and-pier foundations, AOAO master policies and lava-zone realities helps an umpire weigh competing estimates fairly. Our <a href="/property-claim-glossary/">property claim glossary</a> explains many of these terms.</p>
<h3>When appraisers can’t agree on an umpire</h3>
<p>If the two appraisers cannot agree on an umpire, many policies allow either side to ask a court to appoint one. The procedure depends on the policy wording and Hawaii law, and the parties or their counsel usually handle that step. We can be proposed as a candidate, but court appointment is a legal matter outside our role.</p>
<h3>What appraisers can expect from us</h3>
<p>We ask both appraisers for the same information, set the same deadlines for each side and share written submissions with both. Meetings and inspections are open to both appraisers. Communication about the substance of the dispute happens with both sides present or copied, so neither party has reason to question the process.</p>
<h2>Fees and scheduling</h2>
<p>Most appraisal clauses split umpire fees equally between the policyholder and the insurer, with each side paying its own appraiser. We provide our fee terms in writing before accepting an appointment. Inter-island travel for site inspections is scheduled in advance.</p>
<p>Hawaii Insurance Appraiser accepts umpire appointments on residential, condo and commercial claims throughout Oahu, Maui, Kauai, the Big Island, Molokai and Lanai. Appraisers on either side can reach us through the <a href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">contact page</a>. For an overview of how umpires fit into the larger process, see our <a href="/appraisal-process-map/">appraisal process map</a>.</p>
<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who selects the umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>The two appointed appraisers usually choose the umpire together. If they cannot agree, many policies allow either party to ask a court to appoint one.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can an umpire decide whether damage is covered?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Generally no. The umpire weighs the amount of loss on disputed items, and coverage questions are for the parties and, if needed, the courts.</p></div></details><details><summary>How are umpire fees usually split?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies divide umpire fees equally between the policyholder and the insurer, while each side pays for its own appraiser.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related specialties</h2><div class="related"><a href="/appraisal-specialties/appraisal-clause-representation/"><small>Specialty</small><span>Appraisal Clause Representation</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-specialties/homeowner-claim-appraisal/"><small>Specialty</small><span>Homeowner Claim Appraisal</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="curre